<p>A relational database becomes a <strong>service</strong> when other people depend on being able to reach it and trust its answers. Correct tables and transactions are part of that service. So are the people who control access, notice growing queues, patch the engine and recover after the wrong data has been committed.</p>
<p>Consider the shop from <a href="/review/relational-next/">Relational databases</a>. Mina has a paid order, 101, with two item rows. The Order API accepts new orders, a worker arranges fulfilment, and staff run reports. The shop assigns an operating owner for the database and an application owner for what an order means. A database that answers quickly but has lost Mina’s order is failing just as surely as one that refuses connections.</p>
<p>This article follows that service from its operating requirements to a safe incident decision. Read it through before selecting a deeper procedure: the same missing order can lead to a very different recovery from a queue of slow requests.</p>

