<h2 id="define-what-the-service-must-survive">Define what the service must survive</h2>
<p>Start with an application action: “a customer can place an order and later retrieve the order we confirmed”. Three separate promises sit inside it. <strong>Availability</strong> says the action can complete within its allowed time. <strong>Durability</strong> says the acknowledged change survives the failures covered by the commit contract. <strong>Correctness</strong> says the resulting facts still mean what the shop promised. A live replica can preserve an incorrect deletion perfectly.</p>
<p>“Highly available” leaves too much open. Name the failure, the work that must remain possible and the acceptable loss. The shop chooses the following illustrative targets. These are requirements to test, not results of a tested deployment.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The shop’s example failure and recovery requirements"><table><thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Allowed effect</th><th>Mechanism and evidence needed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Reporting demand saturates the service</td><td>Restore order-taking within 10 minutes; lose no acknowledged orders</td><td>Workload controls, headroom and a tested alert-to-intervention path</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Writer host and its storage are lost</td><td>Usable service within 5 minutes; lose no acknowledged orders</td><td>A suitably protected surviving copy, safe writer replacement and measured application reconnection</td></tr>
 <tr><td>A committed mistake is discovered within 7 days</td><td>Correct affected orders within 60 minutes of declaring the incident; account for every acknowledged order</td><td>Retained earlier state, an isolated restore, validation and a plan for valid later work</td></tr>
 <tr><td>The production location or administrative account is lost</td><td>Usable service within 4 hours; at most 5 minutes of acknowledged changes lost</td><td>Independently accessible copies and keys, a replacement environment and a timed recovery exercise</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The allowed recovery duration is often called the <strong>recovery time objective</strong>, or RTO. The allowed gap between the latest required data and the state you can recover is the <strong>recovery point objective</strong>, or RPO. Specify when each clock starts. The shop’s mistake budget begins at incident declaration; its seven-day history requirement accounts for late discovery. A five-minute RPO for total location loss does not make it acceptable to discard five minutes of good orders during a selective repair.</p>
<p>Also set an ordinary service objective against measured application work: which order requests count, their success and latency targets, the observation window and the demand the service is expected to handle. An annual availability percentage cannot tell an on-call engineer whether this particular five-minute outage is acceptable.</p>
<p>Draw the whole recovery path. The API needs a working database endpoint, network route, identity and credentials. Recovery may also need a key service, storage account, extension packages, configuration and a person authorised to provision capacity. Put a database copy in another location but leave its only decryption key or recovery identity in the failed account, and that copy may be unusable when required.</p>

<h3 id="assign-every-operating-responsibility">Assign every operating responsibility</h3>
<p>Self-managed means the shop supplies the database operating work, even when a cloud provider supplies the machine. A managed service takes on specified tasks under its documented contract. It still needs someone at the shop to choose settings, handle alerts and establish that recovered orders are correct.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Operating responsibilities for self-managed and managed databases"><table><thead><tr><th>Responsibility</th><th>Self-managed owner</th><th>Managed-service question</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Patching and engine upgrades</td><td>Database operator plans, executes and validates</td><td>What does the provider apply automatically, when can it interrupt work, and who checks application compatibility?</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Monitoring and escalation</td><td>Operator maintains signals and on-call action</td><td>Which failures trigger provider action, and who handles workload, quota and application alerts?</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Backups, retention and keys</td><td>Operator creates and protects copies and recovery access</td><td>Are copies enabled, what survives deletion or account loss, and who can revoke their keys?</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Failover and restoration</td><td>Operator executes the prepared procedures</td><td>Which transitions are automatic, who can initiate a restore, and what does it leave to the customer?</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Usable, correct application service</td><td>Application owner and incident lead approve the result</td><td>Who reconnects clients, checks business facts and reconciles uncertain or external work?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Give each row a named team, an escalation path and an accessible runbook. For example, Amazon RDS can restore a DB instance to a point in time by creating a <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PIT.html">new instance</a>. The shop still has to check its configuration, validate its data and decide when applications should use it. A provider’s “available” status does not complete that decision.</p>

<h2 id="limit-who-can-reach-and-change-the-data">Limit who can reach and change the data</h2>
<p>Follow an Order API connection through three gates. The <strong>network boundary</strong> decides whether it can reach the database listener. <strong>Authentication</strong> establishes the identity opening the session. <strong>Privileges</strong> decide which objects and operations that identity may use. Passing the first gate does not establish either of the others.</p>
<p>The shop gives the API only the operations needed for ordinary orders. A separate migration identity can change the schema; a reporting identity reads an approved dataset; monitoring and backup identities get the access their tools actually require. An emergency administrator has a controlled, recorded access path. If the ordinary application owns every table or is an administrator, the privilege boundary offers little protection when that application is compromised.</p>
<p>In PostgreSQL, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/auth-pg-hba-conf.html">host authentication rules</a> choose which connections can authenticate and how. Object <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-priv.html">ownership and privileges</a> then govern their authority. Verify allowed and denied actions using the real identities: a successful API login does not prove that it cannot drop the orders table.</p>
<p>Encryption in transit protects the connection; the client must also verify the server identity according to its TLS configuration. Encryption at rest protects stored copies under a key-management contract. Neither removes the authority of an authenticated session allowed to read the data. Audit evidence answers a further question: who used that authority, against what, and when? Keep useful audit records outside the same easy deletion path as the data they explain.</p>
<p>Backups and archived logs are copies of sensitive data too. Include their readers, retention controls, deletion permissions and recovery keys in the boundary. Test rotation and emergency access before an incident forces their first use. <a href="/review/relational-next/security/">Securing a relational service</a> develops these checks and compromise response. The application’s <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#preserve-values-and-authority-when-sending-sql">query and authorisation boundaries</a> remain part of the application design.</p>

<h2 id="observe-the-service-before-it-reaches-a-limit">Observe the service before it reaches a limit</h2>
<p>Start with what people experience: successful order attempts, failed attempts, latency and incoming demand. Then follow the work towards the database. Are requests waiting for a connection, executing, waiting for a lock or waiting for storage? A full connection pool is a <strong>symptom</strong>. Its configured size is a <strong>limit</strong>. A reporting batch occupying every useful worker might be the <strong>cause</strong>.</p>
<p>Compare with a baseline for a similar workload and time of day. Watch connections and active work separately: an open idle connection is different from a query consuming CPU. Connect waits to CPU, memory pressure, storage latency and free space. Include old transaction age and retained versions, transaction-log growth, replica progress and backup health where the deployment exposes them. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/monitoring-stats.html">activity and statistics views</a> expose many of these observations; host and application signals supply the rest.</p>
<p>Capacity is also the time available to act. Suppose the service has 96 GB free, grows by 4 GB an hour and reserves 32 GB for maintenance or intervention. It has 16 hours before reaching that reserve: (96 − 32) ÷ 4. If an archive failure adds 8 GB an hour of retained logs, the same allowance lasts only 5.3 hours.</p>
<p>Figure 1 advances both forecasts through the same elapsed hours. Choose <em>Archive blocked</em> and compare the time remaining with the eight-hour intervention lead time.</p>
<ServiceFigure scene="capacity" number={1} />
<p>The second forecast already demands action at hour zero. Waiting for a universal “90% full” alert could leave less time than provisioning, changing retention safely or restoring elsewhere needs. The rates are invented constant averages; real work can arrive in bursts, so the budget also needs a burst allowance and fresh observations.</p>
<p>Make alerts lead to a decision. “Order latency high” should name an owner and begin with demand, queues and waits. “Archive failing” should begin with the last successful archive and remaining storage time. In PostgreSQL, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html">failed WAL archiving retains log segments</a> locally and can eventually take the service offline. Deleting database log files by hand is not an operating remedy.</p>
<p>Reserve capacity for degraded operation as well as normal peaks. A surviving writer may inherit reads, recovery may replay logs while serving requests, and a backup or rebuild competes for storage bandwidth. <a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">Monitoring and sizing a relational service</a> turns those demands into budgets and alerts. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> when the evidence calls for query or concurrent-workload diagnosis.</p>

<h2 id="make-routine-maintenance-a-controlled-change">Make routine maintenance a controlled change</h2>
<p>Writes leave work behind. In PostgreSQL, updates and deletes leave row versions that <code>VACUUM</code> can reclaim when they are no longer needed. <code>ANALYZE</code> refreshes the statistics used to plan queries. Autovacuum automates these tasks, but its presence does not establish that it is keeping up. Long-lived transactions can hold back cleanup, and retained replication history can hold storage even when application row counts barely change.</p>
<p>That gives an ordinary maintenance decision. If old versions and file growth rise together, first establish what prevents cleanup and whether upkeep has enough resources. A large rewrite may need more space and stronger locks than the service can spare. In PostgreSQL, routine <code>VACUUM</code> primarily makes space reusable inside the table; <code>VACUUM FULL</code> rewrites it under an exclusive lock. “Clean up the table” does not name a safe action until those differences are considered. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html">routine vacuuming documentation</a> defines the engine’s behaviour.</p>
<p>Give any operating change five things: an owner, a precondition, a resource and interruption allowance, signals to watch, and a recovery path. For a patch, verify the supported version and restart behaviour. For a major upgrade, also verify application and extension compatibility, data-format restrictions and the available route back. A backup taken before upgrading is useful only if the intended recovery environment can use it.</p>
<p>Practise with representative data and application work, then stop or revise the change if its observed lock time, errors or resource use exceeds the allowance. <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">Maintaining and upgrading a relational service</a> supplies the operating procedure. Changes to schemas, stored business facts and database locations belong to <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a>.</p>

<h2 id="choose-where-reads-and-writes-can-safely-run">Choose where reads and writes can safely run</h2>
<p>Begin with one writable service and enough capacity for its workload. Add another copy for a named reason. A standby can shorten recovery from writer loss. A readable replica can take suitable read work. A larger writer may solve the current capacity problem with less operating work. None of those choices repairs a committed wrong change.</p>
<p>Follow order 103 from its commit to a later read. With asynchronous replication, the writer can acknowledge it before the replica has it. Losing that writer and its storage at that moment can lose an order the customer was told about. Waiting for a selected standby’s durable copy can protect that failure boundary, but the standby may still need to replay the change before its queries see it.</p>
<p>Figure 2 separates those moments. Advance to the writer flush, switch the acknowledgement policy, then advance to the standby flush and replay. Finally watch an incorrect deletion reach both live copies.</p>
<ServiceFigure scene="copies" number={2} />
<p>The shop’s immediate order-status read belongs on a copy that can see the preceding write. Routing it to the writer is a straightforward starting policy; using a replica requires a supported freshness mechanism or an acceptable stale-read contract. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby.html#SYNCHRONOUS-REPLICATION">synchronous replication settings</a> distinguish standby write, flush and replay acknowledgements. The exact participants and settings matter more than the word “synchronous”. Waiting for a missing required participant can also prevent commits completing.</p>
<p>Replacing the writer is a separate decision. First establish which copy can meet the loss requirement, then exclude the former writer from accepting writes before making another authoritative. A machine you cannot reach may still be reachable by an application elsewhere. Change the connection path, reconnect clients and validate new writes and required reads. Amazon RDS documents that a <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_RebootInstance.html">Multi-AZ DB instance failover changes DNS and requires existing connections to be re-established</a>; a stable endpoint name does not preserve an existing session.</p>
<p>Partitioning also needs a specific problem. Local table partitions can make retention work manageable, but add boundary, index and ongoing creation duties while remaining in one database service. Distributing data ownership across services can spread write demand or place data near its users, but adds routing, cross-owner transactions, movement and coordinated recovery costs. Read replicas alone cannot spread writes that must reach the same owner. <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">Operating replicas and partitioned data</a> develops the topology choice, promotion and placement procedures.</p>

<h2 id="prove-that-the-data-can-be-restored">Prove that the data can be restored</h2>
<p>A <strong>backup</strong> preserves a recoverable representation of data. A <strong>restore</strong> makes a usable database from recovery inputs. <strong>Point-in-time recovery</strong> reconstructs an earlier state using a suitable base copy and retained transaction history. Check the engine’s supported backup contract: a collection of files copied while the engine changes them is not automatically a valid backup.</p>
<p>Choose recovery inputs against the failure. A lost service needs enough state and dependencies to construct a replacement. An accidental deletion needs a state before the deletion. Corruption discovered days later needs an earlier usable copy and checks that the same damage is absent. A replica may already contain either the deletion or the damage, so its survival alone does not answer those cases.</p>
<p>For the shop’s seven-day detection window, retain recovery coverage for the oldest required target, including the base copies and history on which it depends. Keep catalogue metadata, configurations, engine and extension requirements, restore credentials and encryption keys usable from the recovery environment. Protect copies against the named storage, location and administrative failures. Confirm access using the recovery identity, rather than assuming the production administrator will still be available.</p>
<p>A successful backup job proves that a copy operation completed under its checks. It does not measure retrieval, provisioning, decryption, restore, replay, data validation or application reconnection. Time those steps in an isolated environment with representative volume and capacity, keeping recovered workers away from live fulfilment systems. Test known orders, their dependent items, permissions and application actions before declaring success.</p>
<p>Returning the whole service to 09:09 would remove a wrong 09:10 deletion, but it would also remove valid order 106 placed at 09:11. Restore separately so the application owner can compare both histories. Then choose between a controlled whole-service return with accounted-for later work, or a deliberate repair of affected facts. An arbitrary table copy can break relationships and overwrite legitimate changes.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">Backing up and restoring relational data</a> owns the restore and validation procedure. Its result should state the state recovered, elapsed time, actual loss and unresolved gaps. If a rehearsal takes 95 minutes against the shop’s 60-minute target, the design has failed that requirement even though every backup job is green.</p>

<h2 id="recover-the-incident-without-destroying-the-evidence">Recover the incident without destroying the evidence</h2>
<p>At 09:12, customers report trouble. In one case requests time out; in another, paid order 101 has disappeared. The initial response has the same structure: establish impact and ownership, preserve evidence and options, distinguish explanations, contain continuing damage, select a prepared procedure and validate before reopening traffic. The actions inside that structure depend on what failed.</p>
<p>Figure 3 walks both invented incidents together. At each step, compare the evidence that justifies the next action. The clocks illustrate a possible decision sequence; they are not measured recovery times.</p>
<ServiceFigure scene="incident" number={3} />
<p>In the overload case, the operator finds that a scheduled reporting batch raised active work and CPU while lock waits stayed low. Pausing its new work lets the order queue drain. The evidence supports restoring capacity and investigating the reporting budget. Restarting the server first would interrupt every session and remove useful observations without removing the source of excess work.</p>
<p>In the deletion case, the job record identifies a committed cleanup at 09:10, and the replica has applied it. The lead stops that job and coordinates affected writes and fulfilment effects. The restoration owner reconstructs the earlier state in isolation. The application owner compares missing order 101 and its dependent facts with the current service, including valid later order 106, before authorising a checked repair. Promotion would copy the same missing data into the new authority.</p>
<p>These are conclusions from the scenario’s evidence, not default remedies for all timeouts or missing rows. A full pool caused by a blocking transaction needs a different investigation from a full pool caused by CPU demand. If the cause is unclear, capture the current evidence, limit continuing harm and bring in the owner of the next diagnostic step.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Incident evidence and the procedure it should select"><table><thead><tr><th>Observed failure</th><th>First distinction to establish</th><th>Owning procedure</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Saturation, connection queues or lock blockage</td><td>Too much work, a scarce resource or a blocker? Compare demand, active work and waits.</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">Monitoring and capacity</a>, then <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance diagnosis</a></td></tr>
 <tr><td>Storage or other capacity exhausted</td><td>What is growing or retained, how long until the next limit, and which intervention preserves recoverability?</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">Capacity response</a> and the identified <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">maintenance</a> or topology owner</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Compromised access</td><td>Which identities and copies are affected, and is unauthorised work continuing?</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/security/">Containment and access recovery</a>, with restoration if data changed</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Writer or host lost</td><td>Can the former writer still accept writes, and which surviving copy meets the loss bound?</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">Writer replacement</a></td></tr>
 <tr><td>Corruption or committed operator error</td><td>Which state is acceptable, what history covers it, and what valid later work must survive?</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">Isolated restoration and validation</a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Preserve useful logs, statement and wait observations, recent deployment and job records, backup metadata and supported recovery copies before an intervention discards them. Keep transaction history that might be needed to recover the latest valid state. Balance capture with immediate containment: stop an identified destructive process promptly and record what you stopped. Avoid improvised deletion of files, history or copies to make an alarming dashboard green.</p>
<p>Set the return-to-traffic gate before the final switch. The incident lead needs evidence that:</p>
<ul>
 <li><strong>The intended data is present and acceptable.</strong> The chosen recovery boundary is verified, supported integrity checks pass within their stated scope, and affected business relationships and representative records agree. Record any acknowledged changes lost or still uncertain.</li>
 <li><strong>There is one intended write authority.</strong> Former writers and destructive jobs remain excluded. Recovered configuration, keys, privileges and dependencies permit the intended application path.</li>
 <li><strong>The application can use it within the budget.</strong> New connections reach the intended service; a representative write and its required read work; errors, latency and capacity remain acceptable as traffic returns in controlled amounts.</li>
 <li><strong>Uncertain and external work has an owner.</strong> The application owner reconciles interrupted requests, payment and fulfilment effects. A restored order does not recall a parcel already dispatched. Use the <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#recover-according-to-what-the-application-knows">application recovery method</a> for those outcomes.</li>
 <li><strong>The remaining recovery option is preserved.</strong> Keep the comparison copy, decision record and a supported fallback until the result is accepted. Identify who can halt the return if checks fail.</li>
</ul>
<p>Missing history, an unverified target, a second possible writer, a failed business check or an unresolved conflict is a no-go for the affected recovery. State the gap and revise the procedure instead of counting “database accepts connections” as success. Unaffected work may continue only when its boundary and side effects are understood.</p>
<p>Finally record and communicate what actually happened: the affected workflows, interruption interval, recovered state, lost acknowledged changes, reconciliation still outstanding and the next update owner. Distinguish a confirmed zero loss from loss you have not yet measured. After service returns, repair the failed control: the reporting budget, cleanup privileges, restore capacity, missing alert or runbook assumption. Test that repair against the failure that exposed it.</p>

